---
// NOTE: Astro strips the whitespace before an inline element or expression that
// starts a line, so `word\n<b>text</b>` renders as `wordtext`. Keep inline tags
// on the same line as the word before them; test/astro-whitespace.test.ts
// enforces it.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site.ts';
import { url } from '../lib/paths.ts';
import { publicationEntries } from '../lib/publication-data.ts';

// The numbers on this page are read from the same data the site renders, so it
// cannot drift into describing a version of the site that no longer exists.
const selectedCount = publicationEntries.filter((entry) => entry.selected).length;

const sections = [
  ['changing-things', 'Changing things'],
  ['in-the-editor', 'Things in the editor that aren’t obvious'],
  ['publishing', 'Why a change takes a minute'],
  ['publications', 'Publications'],
  ['shelved', 'What isn’t on the site yet'],
  ['signing-in', 'Signing in'],
  ['moving', 'If this ever has to move'],
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How this works"
  description="How the site is put together, and what to do when something looks wrong."
  noindex
>
  <article class="manual">
    <h1>How this site works</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      This page is for you rather than for visitors. It covers where the words on
      this site actually live, why the publication list behaves the way it does,
      and what to do when something looks wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      It isn’t in the menu, so visitors won’t stumble across it. Bookmark it, or
      come back to <a href={url('/how-this-works')}>/how-this-works</a> whenever
      you need it.
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-labelledby="toc-heading">
      <h2 id="toc-heading">On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        {sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="changing-things">
      <h2>Changing things</h2>

      <p>
        Everything you can edit lives at <a href={url('/admin/')}><code>/admin</code></a>.
        You sign in, pick the thing you want to change, type into a form, and save.
        There is no separate copy of the site kept anywhere else, and nothing you
        change here can break the layout.
      </p>

      <dl>
        <dt>Pages</dt>
        <dd>The body text of Home, Research, and Join.</dd>

        <dt>People</dt>
        <dd>
          One entry per person. Adding someone to the lab means adding an entry;
          nothing else needs touching.
        </dd>

        <dt>Site details</dt>
        <dd>
          Your name, job title, department, email, and the footer. There is exactly
          one copy of each of these, used everywhere it appears.
        </dd>

        <dt>Publications</dt>
        <dd>
          One list of every paper. Tick a paper as selected and it also goes in the
          short list at the top of the Publications page, with a sentence about why
          it matters.
        </dd>

        <dt>News and Projects</dt>
        <dd>A post or a project per entry, in whatever order you set.</dd>
      </dl>

      <aside class="note">
        <p>
          Your old site kept its own copy of your job title on every page, and the
          three copies had drifted apart — two said “Infectious Disease
          Epidemiologist” and the third had lost it. That can’t happen now, because
          there is only one copy to change.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section id="in-the-editor">
      <h2>Things in the editor that aren’t obvious</h2>

      <p>
        Most of the forms explain themselves. These are the ones that don’t.
      </p>

      <dl>
        <dt>A publication is in “All publications” whatever you tick</dt>
        <dd>
          Every entry is listed under <cite>All publications</cite>. Ticking <b>Selected publication</b> lists it a second time, at
          the top of the page. The DOI is just the identifier, like <code>10.1038/s41586-024-07626-3</code>, printed on the paper’s page at
          the publisher.
        </dd>

        <dt>“Order” is a number, not a position</dt>
        <dd>
          Lower numbers come first. They don’t have to be consecutive, and leaving
          gaps — 10, 20, 30 — means you can slot something in later without
          renumbering everything else.
        </dd>

        <dt>“Show in navigation” hides a page rather than deleting it</dt>
        <dd>
          The page still exists at its own address and anyone with the link can
          read it. Useful for something you’re still drafting.
        </dd>

        <dt>“Placeholder entry” marks an invented person</dt>
        <dd>
          The stand-in team members are flagged with it, so the site can label
          them as not real on the rare occasion one is visible. Nothing flagged
          this way is published — the whole team section is waiting — and the
          flag goes away when the last stand-in does.
        </dd>

        <dt>“Site details” is a single entry, not a list</dt>
        <dd>
          You can’t create a second one, deliberately. It holds the handful of
          things that appear on every page, so there is only ever one copy to
          change.
        </dd>

        <dt>Anything marked optional can be left empty</dt>
        <dd>
          A person with no photo shows their initials. A project with no funder
          simply doesn’t mention one. Nothing breaks, and no empty labels appear.
        </dd>

        <dt>Uploaded images all go to one place</dt>
        <dd>
          So a photo you added for one thing can be picked again for another
          without uploading it twice.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section id="publishing">
      <h2>Why a change takes a minute</h2>

      <p>
        Saving doesn’t edit a live page. It records your change, and the whole site
        is then rebuilt and republished from scratch. That normally takes one to
        three minutes, so if you save and immediately reload, you’ll still see the
        old version. Wait a couple of minutes and reload again.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you’re working through several pages at once, use <b>Save without
        publishing</b> on each one and then press <b>Publish changes</b> when you’re
        done. The site rebuilds once instead of after every save.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="publications">
      <h2>Publications</h2>

      <p>
        Your publications are a list you keep yourself, in the editor under <b>Publications</b>: one row per paper. Nothing is copied in from
        anywhere, so a paper is on the site when you have added it and not
        before, and what it says is what you typed. There are {publicationEntries.length} papers at the moment, {selectedCount} of them selected.
      </p>

      <h3>Adding a paper</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>On Google Scholar, find the paper, click <b>Cite</b> and then <b>BibTeX</b>, and copy what it shows.</li>
        <li>In the editor choose <b>Publications</b>, open <b>Publication list</b> and add a row at the top of the list.</li>
        <li>Paste the entry into <b>BibTeX entry</b>. The title, authors, journal, year, DOI and link are all read from it, so there is nothing else to type.</li>
        <li>Tick <b>Selected publication</b> if it should also appear at the top.</li>
        <li>
          For a selected paper you can also add an <b>Image</b>, such as a figure or
          graphical abstract. <b>Image caption</b> is the text printed under it, such
          as the figure legend from the paper, and <b>Image description</b> is a short
          sentence saying what it shows for anyone who cannot see it.
        </li>
        <li>Save, then press <b>Publish changes</b>. The list is sorted for you, so a new row can go anywhere.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        One entry to a row. Anything in the entry can be corrected by editing the
        pasted text, which is the way to fix a journal name Scholar has put in the
        wrong case, or to add authors it has cut off. Scholar often stops an author
        list after about ten names and ends it with “and others”; the page only
        prints the first six anyway, and keeps your own name in if you would
        otherwise be cut off, so that is rarely worth fixing. If a row cannot be
        read, the site refuses to publish and says which row and why, rather than
        printing half a citation.
      </p>

      <h3>Things the page does on its own</h3>

      <p>
        Newest first, and alphabetical within a year, so the order never depends on
        when you added something. Long author lists are cut after six, with your
        own name kept in if you would otherwise be cut off, and your name is bold
        wherever it appears. The title links to the paper’s DOI unless the entry has
        a link of its own. Entries from preprint servers such as bioRxiv, medRxiv
        and SSRN are labelled Preprint automatically; tick <b>Preprint</b> if one
        is not recognised.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Why not copy it from ORCID?</summary>
        <p>
          It used to be. The site re-read your ORCID record every morning, which
          meant anything missing from ORCID was missing here too, and the only fix
          was to go and edit ORCID. A list you keep yourself can be corrected on
          the spot, and it can hold papers ORCID has never heard of.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="shelved">
      <h2>What isn’t on the site yet</h2>

      <p>
        The site is Home, Research, Publications, News and Join. Five other sections are
        written and working but switched off, because every one of them is
        currently filled with stand-in content, and a menu item that leads to
        invented people or invented projects is worse than a short menu.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
        <caption>What is on the site, and what is waiting</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Section</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Home, Research</th><td>Live. Your real text, carried over</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Join</th><td>Live. A short note that you will soon be recruiting</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Publications</th><td>Live. A list you keep in the editor</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Site details, footer</th><td>Live. Real</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Team</th><td>Waiting. You are real; everyone else is a placeholder</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Software &amp; data</th><td>Waiting. Your real repositories, but nothing links to them yet</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">News</th><td>Live. Empty until you add the first item</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Projects</th><td>Waiting. Placeholder entries</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Events, Gallery</th><td>Waiting. Fixtures, not yet connected to Google</td></tr>
        </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Nothing has been deleted, and the editor still offers all of it — you can
        write the real News items or fill in the real team long before the section
        goes back on the site. Turning one back on is a two-line change for
        whoever is working on the code; ask, and it is a minute’s work.
      </p>

      <p>
        The placeholder people are named things like “PhD Student Name” on purpose.
        Inventing plausible-looking researchers and putting them on a real
        academic’s website is a bad idea even temporarily, which is most of why the
        team page is waiting rather than live.
      </p>

      <aside class="note">
        <p>
          Two of them will work differently from everything else when they come
          back: instead of being typed into the editor, <b>Events</b> will read a
          Google Calendar and <b>Gallery</b> will read a shared Google Drive
          folder. You already live in Google Calendar, and asking you to re-enter
          a seminar into a second system is a battle nobody wins.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section id="signing-in">
      <h2>Signing in</h2>

      <p>
        The CMS asks for a GitHub access token, which is a long password GitHub
        generates for you. It’s stored only in your browser. It expires — once a
        year at most — and when it does, the CMS simply says you’re signed out.
        Nothing is lost.
      </p>

      <p>
        The instructions for getting a new one are printed at the bottom of
        the <a href={url('/admin/')}>sign-in page</a> itself, because the moment
        you need them is the moment you can’t get in.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="moving">
      <h2>If this ever has to move</h2>

      <p>
        Everything is stored as ordinary text files in one place, and the published
        site is a folder of plain web pages with no software running behind it.
        There’s no company holding your content and no subscription to keep paying.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the site ever needs to move somewhere else — a university server, a
        different host, whatever exists in ten years — the words, the photos and the
        publication list come with it unchanged. That was a deliberate choice, and
        it’s the reason a few things here are slightly more awkward than they’d be
        otherwise. The access token is the main one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
